 {"h": "Who was Pericles?", "paras": [
  "Pericles (about 495–429 BC) came from the old nobility: his mother was a niece of Cleisthenes. Yet he made his career as the leader of the people. He helped strip the old council of the Areopagus of its political powers in 462/1 BC, introduced pay for jurors so that the poor could serve, proposed the law of 451/0 BC limiting citizenship to those with two Athenian parents, and directed the building of the Parthenon. From 443 BC he was elected one of the ten generals every year until his death.",
  "He was famous for his calm and his oratory; comic poets nicknamed him 'the Olympian', like Zeus. He held no office above any other general. His power came entirely from persuasion and re-election. In 'How we got here' you read his funeral oration of 431 BC, the most famous praise of democracy ever spoken. Here we read what happened a year later."
 ], "key": "Pericles, an aristocrat who led the people, held no special office: his power came from persuasion and from being re-elected general fifteen years running."},
 {"h": "Who was Thucydides?", "paras": [
  "Thucydides, son of Olorus, was an Athenian who caught the plague and survived. In 424 BC, as a general, he failed to save the city of Amphipolis from the Spartans and was exiled for twenty years, which gave him time and access to both sides. He set out to write the history of the war from its start, promising accuracy rather than entertainment.",
  "He was not neutral. He admired Pericles and despised Cleon, the popular leader who may have been behind his exile. Our main passage, II.65, looks back from the end of the war, after 404 BC: it is a verdict written with hindsight. Historians take it seriously and question it."
 ], "key": "Thucydides was an exiled Athenian general who wrote the war's history; he admired Pericles, disliked his successors, and wrote II.65 after the war was lost."},
